<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Carpatho-Ukraine</h1><p class="st-meta">1938 CE – 1939 CE · capital Khust</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An autonomous Ukrainian region of Czechoslovakia that declared independence on 15 March 1939 and was conquered by Hungary the same week.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich-agreement">Munich Agreement</a> of 1938 weakened <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Subcarpathian Ruthenia</a> received autonomy in October 1938, first under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andriy-brodiy">Andriy Brodiy</a> and then the Greek Catholic priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avgustyn-voloshyn">Avgustyn Voloshyn</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-vienna-award">First Vienna Award</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzhhorod">Uzhhorod</a> and the southern lowlands to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary</a> (1920-1946), the government moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khust">Khust</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The autonomous government renamed the region Carpatho-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, promoted Ukrainian language and culture and formed a paramilitary force, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-sich">Carpathian Sich</a>, which attracted volunteers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a>. Elections in February 1939 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avgustyn-voloshyn">Voloshyn</a>'s Ukrainian National Union a large majority. The region was poor, mountainous and dependent on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, the Carpatho-Ukrainian diet declared independence on 15 March 1939 and elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avgustyn-voloshyn">Voloshyn</a> president. Hungarian troops invaded the same day and occupied the region within days. It was annexed to the Soviet <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ukrainian-ssr">Ukrainian SSR</a> in 1945.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/carpatho-ukraine.jpg" alt="Carpatho-Ukraine" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: Ιγκόρ · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Ukraine_(1917%E2%80%931921).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1938 CE – 1939 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Khust</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Andriy Brodiy, Avgustyn Voloshyn</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek Catholic and Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ukrainian, Rusyn</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Carpathian Ukraine, Subcarpathian Ruthenia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=carpatho-ukraine" aria-label="Carpatho-Ukraine on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5222 492 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Carpatho-Ukraine lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5542 748L5330 692M5542 748L5445 768"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5330 692h0M5445 768h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5542 748h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5542 748h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Khust. <a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=carpatho-ukraine">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

